WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.489
After decades of silence, the CDC

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:04.559
is speaking up about America's

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.999
gun violence epidemic.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.569
Gun violence in America is

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.609
a public health emergency.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.259
Gun violence is a public health

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:13.739
issue.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:14.831
And we need to start thinking of

00:00:14.832 --> 00:00:16.859
this as a public health crisis.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.719
Republican Congressman Jay Dickey

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:20.609
is infamous for a 1996

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.709
law prohibiting the CDC from using

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:24.479
federal funds to advocate for gun

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.079
control.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.339
The Dickey Amendment.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.719
The so-called Dickey Amendment.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:29.489
Remember the Dickey Amendment, which

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.709
has effectively kept the CDC from

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:33.299
studying gun violence?

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.739
What inspired the Dickey Amendment

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.529
was a blockbuster 1993 CDC-funded

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.209
study, which claimed to show that

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:40.589
owning guns made Americans

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.809
overwhelmingly less safe.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.669
Having a gun in your home not

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.559
only doesn't protect you,

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.409
but it puts your family at risk.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.209
According to a recent film published

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.859
by The New York Times, the NRA

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.209
lobbied for the Dickey Amendment

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:56.159
because the 1993 study made

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.839
an overwhelming case against gun

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.489
ownership, and it wanted to stop the

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:01.409
CDC from ever backing studies

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:03.119
of this sort again.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:05.039
And the NRA didn't think

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:06.359
that that would be good for

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.229
business.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:09.479
And this really spurred

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.339
them on to attack us with much

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:12.959
greater fury.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.489
The problem with this story is that

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.649
the 1993 study doesn't actually

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.539
show what its proponents claim.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.459
It had serious statistical problems

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:21.839
that stemmed from its approach to

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.489
the topic of gun violence,

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:24.689
underscoring why the Dickey

00:01:24.690 --> 00:01:26.399
Amendment was good policy.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.049
A public health agency isn't

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:29.909
well-suited to analyzing social

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.859
science issues of this sort.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:33.329
This is not serious research

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.889
designed to improve individual

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.929
decisions or social policy.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:38.219
It's scientific crap.

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.079
It is used to camouflage arguments

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.699
no one would take seriously if they

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.229
were presented clearly.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.269
Statistician Aaron Brown has taught

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:47.069
at NYU and the University of

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.019
California at San Diego.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.789
He's a columnist for Bloomberg and

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.619
an expert on risk management.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.569
The initial conception of this study

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:56.429
is that a gun is like a pathogen in

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.319
your home that causes homicide

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.179
in unspecified ways.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:02.639
A gun is like a homicide virus.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:04.259
Brown points out that unlike with

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:06.149
death from disease, when a gun

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:07.679
kills someone in a home, the

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.749
surrounding circumstances are highly

00:02:09.750 --> 00:02:10.499
relevant.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.089
The researchers count all deaths

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:13.889
equal to whether the homeowner or

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:16.049
her family or a homicidal

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.219
home invader.

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.929
Someone not blinded by disease

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.609
control thinking would focus on how

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.349
these guns were used for

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:24.539
self-defense, suicide, accidental

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.559
injury, domestic violence, or seen

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.289
by mechanically used against the

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:29.489
owner.

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.079
This could be useful information for

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:32.489
someone weighing the benefits and

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.349
risks of keeping a gun in the house.

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:36.119
A major flaw in the study is that it

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:37.949
attributes homicides to the presence

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:39.239
of guns in the home.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.549
Even if a gun wasn't involved.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:42.809
In fact, the majority of the

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:44.879
homicides don't have a gun at all.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:46.499
And if a gun was involved, the

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.209
researchers don't bother to ask if

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.349
it was to homeowner's gun.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.609
The risk was 200%

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.529
greater that someone in your

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:57.389
family would be shot and killed with

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.369
a gun and the risk that

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:00.869
someone in your home would commit

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:02.789
suicide with the gun was

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.679
five times greater.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.809
The 400% increase

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:07.810
in risk.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:09.989
That may be what Rosenberg would

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:11.849
have liked the study to find, but

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:12.869
it did not.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:14.309
It did find that people living in

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:15.809
homes with guns were murdered in

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.249
their homes more frequently than

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:18.749
people living in similar houses

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.069
without guns.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:22.199
But only 25% more frequently,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.049
not 200% or five times.

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:27.119
And far more dangerous than guns

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:28.859
were living in a gated community,

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.689
living alone or renting.

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.189
Having been in trouble for drinking

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:33.839
at work, made you 20 times more

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.729
likely to be murdered in your home.

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:38.069
The politicized nature of the study

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.109
is shown by ignoring the big risks

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:41.699
it found and highlighting one of the

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:42.959
smallest.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.369
But what this really shows is the

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:46.019
methodology is absurd.

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:47.849
No one would take it seriously if

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.589
we applied it to rent in gated

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.449
communities and no one should take

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.519
it seriously applied to guns.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.319
Brown says that the authors also

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.179
failed to establish convincingly

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.339
that the 25% finding isn't

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.469
the result of random chance.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.299
It is notoriously easy to

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.639
find 25% or 50%

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:07.409
effects of the kind shown in this

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:08.459
paper.

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:10.559
For any effect you want.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.629
The study considered 388 people

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.359
murdered at homes and found that 174

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.309
of them had owned guns.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:19.499
They matched them with 388 similar

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.929
people who were not murdered, 138

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.189
of whom owned guns.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:24.599
The first question you might ask is

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.889
whether this difference could be the

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.779
result of random chance

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.729
if gun ownership has no effect

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:30.740
on your chance of getting murdered

00:04:30.741 --> 00:04:31.741
at home.

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:33.889
Statisticians call this the

00:04:33.890 --> 00:04:36.409
significance of the study.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:38.029
By arbitrary convention.

00:04:38.030 --> 00:04:39.139
Most journals will accept

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:40.969
publications of the significance

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.099
as less than 5%.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:44.509
That is if there is less than one

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:45.743
chance in 20 years or just random

00:04:45.744 --> 00:04:46.744
variation.

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:49.669
The authors compute a 4%

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:51.709
significance for the 174

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:53.749
murder victims with guns.

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:55.459
But this calculation assumes all

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:57.409
data is measured without error,

00:04:57.410 --> 00:04:58.819
that their sampling is perfectly

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:00.619
random, and that all important

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:03.109
control variables are included.

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:04.369
Those assumptions are never met

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:06.289
exactly in any real study,

00:05:06.290 --> 00:05:07.794
and this study is very far from

00:05:07.795 --> 00:05:08.659
ideal.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:10.369
A rough rule of thumb used by many

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:12.289
practicing statisticians

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:14.239
is to be taken seriously

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:16.099
and observational after the fact.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:17.749
Study like this needs to find

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:21.005
a 3-to-1 effect. That is three times as many gun owners murdered in their homes as similar non-gun owners, regardless of idealized statistical calculation.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:29.779
Brown says that when studying topics

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:31.219
like gun violence that are fully

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.139
determined by human behavior,

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:34.309
it's necessary to take a

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:36.319
multidisciplinary approach,

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.209
a requirement that may be lost on

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:39.889
public health scientists trained to

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:41.269
study disease.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:42.889
You fight an epidemic by trying to

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:44.359
eradicate a pathogen.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:45.949
So the only solution to a gun

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.569
violence epidemic is to get rid of

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:48.889
guns.

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:50.359
But violence is a more complex

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.399
social problem than disease.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:53.809
Violence can do good as well as

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:55.789
harm, and guns can protect

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:57.529
as well as kill.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:59.089
In an epidemic, all of this are

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:01.039
equal and all are bad,

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:03.199
but some gun deaths are good,

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:04.429
the result of legitimate

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:06.259
self-defense or law enforcement.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.029
And some are worse than others.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:10.639
Innocent bystanders, for example,

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:12.349
versus suicide or armed gang members

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:13.459
trying to kill each other.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:14.839
Gun control policy should be

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.279
informed by criminologists,

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:17.959
sociologists, psychologists,

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.939
political scientists, legal experts,

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:21.199
historians, and others.

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:22.519
All of these fields have built up

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.259
extensive knowledge about guns and

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:25.109
violence.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:26.869
Yet this study and most public

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:28.729
health gun control research cites

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:30.199
only other public health

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:31.249
researchers.

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:33.709
That NRA strategy

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:36.229
of hyperpolarizing the people

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:38.509
worked out well for them,

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:40.339
but it had devastating

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:42.649
consequences in terms of continuing

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:43.699
gun deaths.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:45.439
The Times video asserts that not

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:47.059
enough research has been done on the

00:06:47.060 --> 00:06:49.009
causes of gun violence and that

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:50.989
Dickey is partly to blame.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:52.579
The real issue, Brown says, is

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.679
quality, not quantity.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:56.119
The Dickey Amendment did not stop

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:57.529
gun control research.

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:59.089
The RAND Corporation tabulated

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:01.039
28,000 recent papers

00:07:01.040 --> 00:07:03.169
and gun control proposals.

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:05.059
The problem is quality.

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:07.129
Only 123 of those papers

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:09.739
met minimal statistical standards.

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:11.569
He said, "isn't it true that

00:07:11.570 --> 00:07:13.579
you really want to get rid

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:15.529
of all guns?" And I

00:07:15.530 --> 00:07:17.509
felt, How dare him?

00:07:17.510 --> 00:07:19.519
This guy is really bad.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:22.369
Worse than not understanding.

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:23.449
He doesn't care.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:26.089
Jay Dickey and I, at that point,

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.489
were really kind of mortal enemies.

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:29.899
The film is structured around the

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:31.729
story of an improbable friendship.

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:33.769
In the 1990s, Congressman Dickey

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:35.569
and Mark Rosenberg, then director of

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:37.729
the CDC's National Center for Injury

00:07:37.730 --> 00:07:39.679
Prevention and Control, went from

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:41.719
adversaries to best friends.

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:43.879
We started talking, and we actually

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.699
started to trust each other,

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.949
and we started to like each other.

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:50.899
And over the years, that

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.849
developed into an incredible

00:07:52.850 --> 00:07:54.379
friendship.

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:56.059
Rosenberg convinces him to change

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:57.769
his mind and repent.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:07:59.959
He is the former self-described NRA

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:01.729
point person and now is a strong

00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:03.769
supporter of research.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:05.629
I think the CDC should do the

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:07.459
gun research that relates

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:08.419
to gun violence.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:09.289
You're the guy.

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:10.459
It's your bill.

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:11.779
Feel different now than I did

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:12.199
before.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:13.909
Dickey, who died of Parkinson's in

00:08:13.910 --> 00:08:15.889
2017, may have come

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:17.389
to regret pushing for the amendment

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:19.309
that bears his name, but he remained

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:21.199
concerned about the 1993

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:23.119
study, which would have complicated

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:24.889
the New York Times narrative in

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:27.079
which the filmmakers left out.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:28.819
The New York Times film uses this

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:29.389
clip.

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:30.289
What changed?

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:31.129
How did you have the change of

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:32.130
heart?

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.699
Well, I just think it's it's

00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:36.319
just the weight of all of the

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.359
incidents that have occurred.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:40.399
And that is that the kids and

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:42.379
the innocent people who are being

00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.209
killed deserve our

00:08:44.210 --> 00:08:44.899
attention.

00:08:44.900 --> 00:08:46.399
What the filmmakers cut out was

00:08:46.400 --> 00:08:48.229
Dickey's ongoing concerns about the

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.059
1993 study and how it was

00:08:50.060 --> 00:08:50.869
carried out.

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:52.729
We wanted research

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:54.559
done for gun

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:56.389
violence, and that's what the

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:57.619
money was paid for.

00:08:57.620 --> 00:09:00.289
But we found out as we went along

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:02.599
that not only was the research

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:04.609
being done just to support gun

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:06.709
control, but we weren't even

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:08.539
given access to what the collected

00:09:08.540 --> 00:09:09.889
data was.

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:11.779
So it was it was clear

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.249
that we needed to do something to

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:15.109
stop what was being done.

00:09:15.110 --> 00:09:16.759
That's exactly correct.

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:18.799
Research funded by the CDC on gun

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:20.479
violence is unscientific from the

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:21.529
start.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:23.509
Guns are not a pathogen and

00:09:23.510 --> 00:09:25.039
violence is not a disease to be

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:26.040
controlled.

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:28.639
What is needed is not propagandistic

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:30.349
films or sloppy research.

00:09:30.350 --> 00:09:32.239
It's cross-disciplinary research on

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:33.559
what drives human beings to

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:35.720
violence, whether with a gun or not.

